#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083201 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083003 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}] :question/id #uuid "714c93f9-6170-52a9-849b-2e9d94805c4f" :question/math-academy-id "e-513" :question/problem "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_1^\\infty \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x} }\\,\\textrm{d}x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{-2} \\frac{1}{(1+x)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^3} \\,\\textrm{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\n- For the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}{x}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[2{x}^{1/2}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[\\sqrt{x}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[\\sqrt{b}-1] \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\n- For the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{1+x}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[-\\frac{1}{1-2}+\\frac{1}{1+a}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{1+a}+1] \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent (not divergent).\n\n- For the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[-\\frac{1}{2{b}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2({1}^{2})}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2{b}^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent (not divergent).\n\nIn conclusion, only integral I is divergent."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050490}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083832 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23acf82b-e58d-58e8-851e-1bcf8b34a403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460107} {:answer/id #uuid "cf6485ad-62f1-5cc1-845b-f8acead5d2d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460108}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf6485ad-62f1-5cc1-845b-f8acead5d2d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460108} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc3c2e9c-4064-5705-bb22-4da95d61f120" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460920} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f30616f-ab91-58ef-a4d5-ee0fe6b58990" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460109} {:answer/id #uuid "e7418585-e683-5057-9c73-a1c0c5846fb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7418585-e683-5057-9c73-a1c0c5846fb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460110} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c3c2d32-ecdd-580f-886b-8931da49ff10" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460921} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "026ffc31-daed-530c-b15b-74e8ab85c121" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460111} {:answer/id #uuid "e220e77a-c7e1-51e6-b067-3d2066d61dc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "026ffc31-daed-530c-b15b-74e8ab85c121" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460111} :answer-field/id #uuid "76d0ef17-3ba8-5540-9bf6-1a11258717fe" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "998df1b1-461f-5fc4-87ac-c188c53c68e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-6032" :question/problem "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{10}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{10}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{10}^{a}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[2\\sqrt{x-1}]}_{10}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a-1}-2\\sqrt{10-1}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a-1}-6) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|-1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|a|) \\\\  & =-\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{a}+\\frac{1}{1}) \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only integrals I and II are divergent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083837 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a05f443-cd9a-598f-a9ae-c4349667382f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\ln (2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081954} {:answer/id #uuid "df3a4679-7471-577a-861c-2625ed7ffca0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(\\ln (2))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081955} {:answer/id #uuid "468596aa-3bff-52e3-a8fe-04acbcbe758a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081956} {:answer/id #uuid "5e0ce69d-d45d-56fb-936d-2c902f48ecce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081957} {:answer/id #uuid "72633a19-e145-5976-9043-e1c1144756e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081958}] :answer-field/id #uuid "be0c1132-fc63-58d6-b4f4-f42202ba3f5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081953} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd9b6d3c-0057-574c-87f0-e4101749ea2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377616} {:answer/id #uuid "3737c8d2-a0da-5074-ba6f-697e878e8a8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377617} {:answer/id #uuid "d2c32b1a-b4cd-59d4-99ae-00c65bacae91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377618} {:answer/id #uuid "d2acd056-6854-53f2-9f3f-d23ce96038ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377619} {:answer/id #uuid "1eac7a83-db29-5df2-847e-ba35f30a046a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2c32b1a-b4cd-59d4-99ae-00c65bacae91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377618} :answer-field/id #uuid "88a03e6d-8814-51d2-8edb-239663fe528e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "322fb4b3-6749-5356-82bf-9541f4f0d3ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-6294" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2a+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1|) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2a+1|) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\infty ) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237800 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21b7bac2-7c49-5bca-b0a4-69dc3a8c9bcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236035} {:answer/id #uuid "440f0c7e-4732-5a58-8653-34d1d6a4c23f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236255} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d494d7-8ebd-5802-8686-db43d5ff7fbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236949} {:answer/id #uuid "c46ac0dc-03a9-5a1a-8f79-a9bea31a3de9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237059} {:answer/id #uuid "dac1ee25-c1c3-53e3-b4cf-2ad6e22e94c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "440f0c7e-4732-5a58-8653-34d1d6a4c23f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236255} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1733310-60f1-5249-b1c5-9809a13bbc72" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "702f5890-f962-5297-b8db-429117ade5e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-100642" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{2z+1}}\\,\\text{d}z=$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{2z+1}}\\,\\text{d}z & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{2z+1}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{3}{2}(2z+1)^{2/3}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[(2z+1)^{2/3}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }((2a+1)^{2/3}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237801 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c33a18d-104d-52e9-9c2f-5e801a820664" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235881} {:answer/id #uuid "2c8d46d3-212e-5d20-a7db-1772936c7f05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236096} {:answer/id #uuid "3e50bf74-aff8-5e86-9467-5aa0194a6d09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236209} {:answer/id #uuid "468b0303-942b-5eae-80d1-6f45657de69f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236271} {:answer/id #uuid "de9f84f3-7d6b-52fd-87cc-c8e2ef3fd885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c33a18d-104d-52e9-9c2f-5e801a820664" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235881} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5392877-e39b-5b6d-8019-6b4895e6d441" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8508ef3-0736-5531-a2ca-192705b437b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-100643" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{2-3t}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{2-3t}} & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{2-3t}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{2-3t}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\sqrt{2+3}-\\sqrt{2-3a}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\sqrt{2-3a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}+\\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237855 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff5263a7-1895-5085-8df6-00b5e700d455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461171} {:answer/id #uuid "6a138658-7727-5b84-9147-e75c4bc6f6be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff5263a7-1895-5085-8df6-00b5e700d455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461171} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fe9a7c8-6e85-594b-b112-88855ef364c3" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462339} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a49961ff-3ffb-57ca-a127-a6af05c42338" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461173} {:answer/id #uuid "283b72c8-abd2-59aa-94d2-c526caba130c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a49961ff-3ffb-57ca-a127-a6af05c42338" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461173} :answer-field/id #uuid "de28bf36-f83a-5fcb-b309-eca4f69c6915" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462340} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a089b8d-46c8-5953-a6f9-b2962253f826" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461175} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3d8c52-3f24-5cde-b9fc-ebd2788b23f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad3d8c52-3f24-5cde-b9fc-ebd2788b23f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461176} :answer-field/id #uuid "292bf9b3-4412-5dd3-80d1-271235a3924d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e90080f-3ef7-5c8d-a0d7-de79fffa1eb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-101735" :question/problem "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u$\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(z+3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z$\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{2(-2)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2{a}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2{a}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(z+3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{(z+3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{z+3}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{a+3}+\\frac{1}{0+3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|]_{2}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡a-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nIn conclusion, only integrals III is divergent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237856 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11cbf150-b683-51fa-9f8a-d203bcfb3ad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461177} {:answer/id #uuid "5956e27d-8d17-501a-8856-f798cc05f016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11cbf150-b683-51fa-9f8a-d203bcfb3ad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461177} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c8fa3d2-c067-5b43-a12b-85e271c9cb1e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462342} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4de3cb94-3a2c-5891-aae8-6ea29f0d4df4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461179} {:answer/id #uuid "8565509e-ebc2-5ae6-96ae-9d8817a43af7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8565509e-ebc2-5ae6-96ae-9d8817a43af7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461180} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f996994-3b27-587f-9d4f-5527cd7a6f32" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462343} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70ebaee1-04f7-54b6-af95-172ae288615f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461181} {:answer/id #uuid "4626a4f4-a591-579d-a987-b3940f00bbae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4626a4f4-a591-579d-a987-b3940f00bbae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461182} :answer-field/id #uuid "81f2e71e-76d6-53a1-bb19-e51bae15960a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462344}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30834ba5-7de3-5c58-8e09-a32166c98581" :question/math-academy-id "q-101738" :question/problem "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u$\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}t\\,\\text{d}t$\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x-2}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{u-1}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1+\\frac{1}{a-1}) \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}t\\,\\text{d}t & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(0-{a}^{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{a}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x-2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x-2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[4\\sqrt{x-2}]}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =4{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\sqrt{a-2}-\\sqrt{3-2}) \\\\  & =4{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\sqrt{a-2}-1) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\nIn conclusion, only integrals II and III are divergent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3447b210-752f-58bc-ae21-6727e68e220b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285271} {:answer/id #uuid "68cdc6da-3b7a-57fc-8b9f-cd3018eea1a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285751} {:answer/id #uuid "6d97721e-a156-5007-a747-5f849a77796c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285798} {:answer/id #uuid "888fbc31-5edc-5fc6-ba9c-9dca4d6a73b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286040} {:answer/id #uuid "ed4d35d0-edf6-5b04-974a-d41a0d358490" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3447b210-752f-58bc-ae21-6727e68e220b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285271} :answer-field/id #uuid "43979b98-c797-55a1-8e56-30f10ae875af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c15a1f53-63be-5a09-b32e-0a9e0c9c95b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-20700" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 2 & \\infty  \\\\ u & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{a}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡u]}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{ln}⁡a-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)] \\\\  & =\\infty -\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332945 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0864cdf9-1ffd-56c1-8cec-9c7e3634c2e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330021} {:answer/id #uuid "757f2c97-4c6e-5a4b-b58e-740ddec39baa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331005} {:answer/id #uuid "c02ca5ed-e5be-530f-b339-6203cf61077f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331655} {:answer/id #uuid "d82819b4-466e-564f-a4fd-bc1c368333eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331867} {:answer/id #uuid "e39d5946-f20c-5fd9-9c4f-e13376e2ce60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0864cdf9-1ffd-56c1-8cec-9c7e3634c2e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330021} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b6c3b91-0868-5cbe-bd0f-5ddc36c6f516" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "06fd8b41-8200-5edd-a18c-2621495e1f13" :question/math-academy-id "q-5964" :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{4/5}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{x}^{4/5}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({a}^{4/5}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot \\infty  \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332961 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36b26abc-59e4-5f26-a879-c88bd9674a2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330441} {:answer/id #uuid "5930fa44-50f4-5443-816a-d851a83c93bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330749} {:answer/id #uuid "7cf921ac-f569-5c86-91ea-21e2d7197b21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331051} {:answer/id #uuid "a448ae0d-5a00-5f30-af7e-73eeb7091140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331425} {:answer/id #uuid "dea4729f-fa01-5b4a-98f1-da79364f0332" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a448ae0d-5a00-5f30-af7e-73eeb7091140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331425} :answer-field/id #uuid "18eb5fcf-2f7e-55f1-9f7e-384542e03025" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4d9a9729-71fc-57cb-8bba-051918208f5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-6034" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}z}{z}.$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}z}{z} & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{6}^{a}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{z} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡z]}_{6}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡a-\\operatorname{ln}⁡6) \\\\  & =\\infty -\\operatorname{ln}⁡6 \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348367 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13276adc-5e19-53a0-9f9a-51eb15d41b59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345516} {:answer/id #uuid "23ba0748-be66-5c10-9740-7a5fc964cba0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345679} {:answer/id #uuid "705951cd-43f5-516c-9a5f-baf621993227" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346332} {:answer/id #uuid "e20fc376-1370-5720-9da8-c277fecbffc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347337} {:answer/id #uuid "f17275a5-42e9-5faa-a693-6409875653d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23ba0748-be66-5c10-9740-7a5fc964cba0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345679} :answer-field/id #uuid "20fa39a2-e338-57a1-ae53-4506e3856b9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347661}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "027c33f1-e979-5d6a-94a5-ba6a2c9a56e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-80342" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\sqrt{1-z}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\sqrt{1-z}} & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\sqrt{1-z}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-2\\sqrt{1-z}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\sqrt{1-0}-\\sqrt{1-a}) \\\\  & =-2+2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\sqrt{1-a} \\\\  & =-2+2\\cdot \\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348368 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cae36fd-3deb-5b7b-8b36-9f9f8dfee127" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345599} {:answer/id #uuid "583455da-d6e1-5029-a452-b73277cbbf12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346105} {:answer/id #uuid "613d7756-c42b-5e58-8499-7e22134bae89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346194} {:answer/id #uuid "7b39e153-c534-5ace-84e4-fe8c0f3ea308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346428} {:answer/id #uuid "a987b942-fa10-5bee-b0d4-7db1e3099b24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a987b942-fa10-5bee-b0d4-7db1e3099b24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346830} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fc125c1-0b75-5549-98a7-4582698ca44e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bc51916f-5f62-552c-9209-e6b4fd87da10" :question/math-academy-id "q-80347" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{8-{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=8-{x}^{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,-\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=8-{x}^{3}$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & -1 & -\\infty  \\\\ u & 9 & \\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{8-{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\infty }^{9}(-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{u}})\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{9}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{8-{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{9}^{a}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[2\\sqrt{u}]}_{9}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\sqrt{a}-\\sqrt{9}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc0f0bbf-9e98-54b3-bf3e-86d3585281f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468852} {:answer/id #uuid "0364d1e1-fd92-5dce-b6af-859298fcbb61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0364d1e1-fd92-5dce-b6af-859298fcbb61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468853} :answer-field/id #uuid "56a22fa4-849c-5c90-9822-62f2fcf23aaf" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470038} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "308906a6-4c6a-54ce-a9d9-4762d925306b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468854} {:answer/id #uuid "d4f5d776-8ae6-5de7-96df-59aa6fc9e843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4f5d776-8ae6-5de7-96df-59aa6fc9e843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468855} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cdd4872-c6a0-59bf-8e3c-aadb70e9ae28" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470039} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1935d638-cd1f-5a78-8128-5b81e02f9c8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468856} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5e324c-feff-5279-9a68-2ee89b03c35d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1935d638-cd1f-5a78-8128-5b81e02f9c8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468856} :answer-field/id #uuid "b36b9451-aa6f-5293-a01d-78f77c5a1f3f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470040}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ebe5f91b-e612-5062-b7df-947a793c33b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-101734" :question/problem "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-5}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-5}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{6}^{a}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[2\\sqrt{x-5}]}_{6}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a-5}-2\\sqrt{6-5}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a-5}-2) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3}\\,{x}^{-2/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{x}^{1/3}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\sqrt[3]{a}-1) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1+\\frac{1}{a}) \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only integrals I and II are divergent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369964 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd4a6913-c932-5739-8608-9cc06b956797" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369966} {:answer/id #uuid "5b620ce7-5cea-5d5e-b6ee-c0c245ff836e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369967} {:answer/id #uuid "cb6badc9-86fe-508a-9845-ba404048d557" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369968} {:answer/id #uuid "1802544e-90f5-58a5-97c6-5c3fad71b6e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369969} {:answer/id #uuid "b1446da5-03f2-5f98-8109-b170d5293fac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd4a6913-c932-5739-8608-9cc06b956797" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369966} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5cb1ae6-80cc-5989-a4fa-22e6a9f2c9c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "94bad8ac-08e4-5bfe-8807-f8590eb5dbd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-20698" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3x+1}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{3x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3a+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3a+1|) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (\\infty ) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399350 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afdb2771-62f0-54fa-9623-4972845d19a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470643} {:answer/id #uuid "29258b68-aef8-521d-bcd0-4ced0913a87b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afdb2771-62f0-54fa-9623-4972845d19a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470643} :answer-field/id #uuid "84edb204-5bb6-5b15-bce3-ab37bb9167a4" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471827} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "671dc28a-4f0a-5152-a704-c6f85c3291ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470645} {:answer/id #uuid "86897aab-f9f4-534b-a305-6d708d258191" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86897aab-f9f4-534b-a305-6d708d258191" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470646} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa7980c7-726d-5b91-94fd-76106f224eca" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471828} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16a0d9e7-b5e7-588d-816d-4ab61e18e992" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470647} {:answer/id #uuid "e352800d-a7f4-56b9-b333-aa4cd4f14a95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16a0d9e7-b5e7-588d-816d-4ab61e18e992" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470647} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a1f5d5e-e005-55bf-8ec5-9de9792267ea" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "79e7ca1d-1ed8-5888-8523-0ec8ed90db1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-101736" :question/problem "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u$\nII. ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}3{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z$\nIII. ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2(u-1)^{2}}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{2(a-1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2(2-1)^{2}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2(a-1)^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}3{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}3{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{z}^{3}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{a}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only integral II is divergent."}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}]}
